  {
    "episode_id": 77325,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77325.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 88,
    "turn_text": "I think in some instances our generic brand positives and negatives are right around Trump levels. And in some areas of the country, they're worse than Trump. And unfortunately, if we're going to win the house back, we are going to have to run in districts where they're in the South, they're in the industrial Midwest, or out West, like the Montana race. And we've got to figure out how to win in those areas. And I don't say this with any joy. I mean, I just want our colleagues to know that You know, what we're fighting about here in the talk about Russia that overwhelms the discussion today and what's happened in Washington is not connecting to people who are worried about whether or not they're going to get their mortgage paid or they're going to get their pension or what their energy bills look like or how they're going to take care of a sick family member. I mean, it's just disconnected from what normal people and average people are going through. And if we don't recognize that, I mean, when Trump is that negative and his negatives are so bad and yet these Republican candidates are still winning, you know, I'm not here to get a second place trophy. I'm not here for a moral victory. You got to win and you got to figure out how to do it and we're just not quite figuring out how to crack the code on some of these things.",
