    "turn_text": "Yeah, it's, it's almost, you know, every quarter hour, you gotta like check in and and take some deep breath, but Dan's great and I love this book and I love all the work he's been doing, but I tell you, not as a joke. I mean, people are really stressed out now. I think watching a very high level of anxiety, and I don't try not to bring it up, but I just have noticed more and more people using the word, everybody's getting stressed out, there's too much anxiety. We're all basically, when you're president of the United States, your kind of mindset, I guess, is projected out into the public, and that's what you do. If President Kennedy's calm during the Cuban Missile Crisis, we feel better about things, whether it's Reagan or whoever, you take on the president's almost a reflection of the country and it works both ways and I think we have really gotten into the Donald Trump drama and we're all living it every single day and it's getting very stressful for people so when guys like Dan or some of these other CEOs or athletes are talking about how some of these contemplative practices could be very helpful and keeping our stress levels down and keeping our perspective on things I think it's really important and that's kind of what I mentioned a few minutes ago about, you know, you can have anger and you can get mad at Donald Trump and that anger should then make us aware of something else that's happening and we should then refocus, okay, I'm mad. What's the best thing I could do with the anger that I have right now? Let's let it go and focus on talking to working class people about you know that and let them know that i'm thinking about them or the war thinking about them in their issues that i'm not so overwhelmed with my personal anger to donald trump that i've forgotten you and i think it's important in a great practice that that dan harris is one of the the great leaders in the country about letting people know and how to do that and stay a little 